{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 3.0,
 "footer": "Real situations \u00b7 Tax Secrets Canada \u00b7 General information, not tax advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "She takes home nine thousand eight hundred dollars a month. And every single Monday, one thousand dollars goes into her T F S A."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Sounds like the most responsible person you know, right?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Here's the problem. If she keeps doing exactly that, sometime in twenty twenty-seven the C R A starts charging her one percent. Every month."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "This story is based on a real situation a Canadian shared online. We changed the name and some numbers. This is general information, not tax advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "But by the end of this video, you'll know exactly what she should do, and what you'd do in her place."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Let's call her Maya. Maya is twenty-seven, she lives in Mississauga, and she works two good jobs. One during the day, one on the side."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "No kids, single, no car."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "After tax, both jobs together put about nine thousand eight hundred dollars a month into her account. That's a great income at twenty-seven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Her goal is simple: keep all her spending around four thousand five hundred a month."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Rent is fifteen hundred, because she has roommates. But every month, she goes over."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Eating out. Shopping. The little things that never feel big on the day you buy them."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "So she did what a lot of smart people do. She opened a T F S A and set up an automatic transfer. One thousand dollars, every Monday."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "And honestly? That part is great. Paying yourself first is the single best habit in this whole story."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "But she wrote that she still feels like she's not using her income to its full potential. And she's right. Just not for the reason she thinks."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Because a thousand a week is fifty-two thousand dollars a year. And a T F S A has a ceiling."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "So the real question isn't how do I invest. It's this: when does the room run out, and what happens the week after?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's what the rules say. For twenty twenty-six, the T F S A dollar limit is seven thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "But room you never used doesn't disappear. It carries forward."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Your room is this year's limit, plus unused room from past years, plus anything you withdrew last year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "And if you go over? Any excess amount in your T F S A is taxed at one percent per month."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "It's calculated on the highest excess in your account that month. Not the average. The highest."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So let's do Maya's math. If you're a resident of Canada, your room starts to build the year you turn eighteen."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Maya turned eighteen in twenty seventeen."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Add up every limit from twenty seventeen to twenty twenty-six. Five thousand five hundred, twice. Then six thousand for four years. Six thousand five hundred. And seven thousand for three years."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Total: sixty-two thousand five hundred dollars of room, if she never contributed before this year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "In twenty twenty-six she puts in fifty-two thousand. That leaves ten thousand five hundred."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "On January first, twenty twenty-seven, she gets a new year of room. The C R A announces each new limit, so for this example we'll use seven thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "That's seventeen thousand five hundred of room for all of twenty twenty-seven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "At about four thousand three hundred and thirty-three a month, that's her thousand a week, the room is gone by the start of May. In May, she's over."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "And if nobody notices until December, the excess climbs to thirty-four thousand five hundred dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "One percent of the highest excess, every month from May to December, adds up to about one thousand five hundred and forty-seven dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Paid on money that was supposed to be tax-free."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "And here's the sneaky part. The T F S A information in your C R A account is only updated once a year, in the spring. It doesn't know what you put in last month."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "And if you save at more than one bank, each bank only sees its own deposits. Only you see the whole picture."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "But the T F S A isn't the only surprise coming for Maya. There's a second trap, and it's sitting on her paycheques."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "When you start a job, you fill out a T D one form. Each employer withholds tax as if that job were your only income."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "The C R A says it plainly: you can't claim tax credits on more than one T D one form if you earn more than the credits you claim."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Or your employers may not take enough tax from your pay, and you can end up with a large, unexpected amount owing."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Two good jobs. Two employers. One surprise bill in April."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So here's the fix, step by step."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Step one. Fix the T D one at the second job. Don't claim the credits twice, and fill out a new T D one to ask that employer to deduct extra tax from each pay."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Step two. Before the next Monday transfer, check your room, and keep your own log of every deposit you make this year, at every bank."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Step three. If Maya wants to buy a home one day, she can open a First Home Savings Account."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "The room is eight thousand dollars a year. Contributions are deductible, like an R R S P. And a qualifying withdrawal to buy a first home is tax-free."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "But the room only starts in the year she opens the account. Waiting costs her room."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Step four. At her income, look hard at the R R S P."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "New R R S P room each year is eighteen percent of last year's earned income, up to thirty-three thousand eight hundred and ten dollars for twenty twenty-six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Step five. Once the T F S A, the F H S A and the R R S P are handled, anything left can go to a regular investment account. Just know that growth there is taxable."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Let's see why step four matters."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Say Maya moves ten thousand dollars of her savings from the T F S A plan into her R R S P instead."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "An R R S P contribution lowers her taxable income. At an illustrative forty percent marginal rate, that's roughly four thousand dollars back."
  },
  {
   "id": "L52",
   "file": "vo/L52.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Same ten thousand saved. But four thousand of it came back from the C R A. Your real rate depends on your income and your province, so check yours before you decide."
  },
  {
   "id": "L53",
   "file": "vo/L53.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Now put it side by side. Without the fix: about one thousand five hundred and forty-seven dollars of excess tax, and a surprise bill in April."
  },
  {
   "id": "L54",
   "file": "vo/L54.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "With the fix: no excess tax, no surprise, and about four thousand dollars back from the R R S P."
  },
  {
   "id": "L55",
   "file": "vo/L55.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So what can you steal from Maya's story?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L56",
   "file": "vo/L56.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "One. Automatic saving is a superpower. Keep it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L57",
   "file": "vo/L57.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Two. The T F S A has a ceiling, and only you are tracking how close you are."
  },
  {
   "id": "L58",
   "file": "vo/L58.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Three. Two jobs means two T D one forms. Fix the second one before April fixes it for you."
  },
  {
   "id": "L59",
   "file": "vo/L59.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now I want to hear from you. If you had five thousand dollars left over every month, where would the first dollar go? F H S A, R R S P, or T F S A? Tell me in the comments."
  },
  {
   "id": "L60",
   "file": "vo/L60.mp3",
   "gap": 0.4,
   "text": "Next week: her tax preparer made one mistake, and she got a twenty thousand dollar bill. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
